#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199266} :activity/id #uuid "64d036ec-bd13-5db0-909a-44c4f2ba1ce6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198234 :question/id #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433504}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "2f924b6e-2f47-553e-b2e6-6d98b8c55641" :step/math-academy-id 26205 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199450 :tutorial/content "Consider a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n![](images/fe/fefb7b82a6da1421bb96798fe6377e66199b50b8630dc396518927a0f3fe204b.svg)\n\nThe points in the $(u,v)$ plane where $\\mathbf T$ is *locally* non-invertible are known as **critical points**. \n\nWe've already seen that $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible when the Jacobian determinant is zero. Therefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ are the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nLet's compute the critical points of the following transformation:\n\n![](images/8e/8e55b2da9a0c1fcf1ab901d361c50b652c906b92f0fcd4b158793f7782cfe5f4.svg)\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =4u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4u & -6v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4u\\cdot 1-(-6v)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when the Jacobian equals zero. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u-3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ consist of those points that lie on the line $2u-3v=0$  in the $uv$-plane, shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5c544e3d3ecbdeaf7dc372b40ee0f37cb14e3ba292b8180a263c91103a9efe61.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f47e688-774b-5c89-a90d-ead52a871598" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4312 :tutorial/title "Critical Points of Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "16b2c818-03e6-5801-97b2-0ce8d225c3d5" :step/math-academy-id 26206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199460 :tutorial/content "Let's now state the so-called **inverse function theorem** for single-variable functions:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the graph below:\n\n![](images/4d/4dfd8b142b594a2ca4f0e39f9b8ca1e04018bf0efd63f9dc2235bd61e9f9d70e.png)\n\nIn this example, the function $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ and has a nonzero derivative. As a result, $f^{-1}$ exists in a neighborhood of $x=b$ and is continuously differentiable at this point.\n\nOn the other hand, if $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ but $f'(a) = 0,$ then one of two things can happen:\n\n- **Case 1**: The function $f$ does not have a local inverse at $a.$\n\n![](images/04/04ee310e6068e517aec42a67fa83837986f8807e1250b89b52e7a62e3975bb38.png)\n\nIn the example above, $f$ is not one-to-one in a neighborhood of $a,$ so it's not invertible there.\n\n- **Case 2**: The function $f$ has a local inverse at $a,$ but its inverse is not continuously differentiable at $b.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e2738a16f999d503fb295f4fd6535a854884468a4d190ffa537a4ec9295490c.png)\n\nIn the example above, $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a,$ but $f^{-1}$ is not differentiable at $x=b$ since it has a vertical tangent at this point." :tutorial/id #uuid "c45037f7-31c7-5911-a00d-edaa4c5b86e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7036 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54cb06ef-1ffc-5b15-9147-a1baf70b985c" :step/math-academy-id 42404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198012 :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486385}] :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "1b07afe2-9993-5d3c-bfe7-b98917224b3b" :step/math-academy-id 42423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199477 :tutorial/content "The inverse function theorem can be generalized to transformations too. We state it below:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf T:U \\to \\Bbb R^2$ be a $C^1$ (continuously differentiable) transformation on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ where *\n![](images/82/829c083606df36014d5b5b078b80c1c0ff1edb72f981880b4a1a6a16affb32dc.svg)\n\n*Further, suppose that $(u_0,v_0)$ is **not** a critical point of $\\mathbf T.$* Then,\n\n- *$\\mathbf T$ is locally invertible at $(u_0,v_0),$*\n\n- *$\\mathbf T^{-1},$ the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ is a $C^1$ function in a neighborhood of $(x_0,y_0),$ and*\n\n- *the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} ,$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04daa2a7-b824-543f-b8d6-79a5b54e4799" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7678 :tutorial/title "The Inverse Function Theorem for Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "63b8e23b-b4a6-5984-b37f-dab9c1ced3af" :step/math-academy-id 44589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198042 :question/id #uuid "217fe5bd-4ea4-57b9-8772-bb6e517207bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049731}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479856}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "809a242a-d30e-5e4d-9b3e-fc6725358aac" :step/math-academy-id 44598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199249}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198489}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407322} :activity/id #uuid "7eb720ac-5358-5a94-bfe5-d2ea9292b4f9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879a74f3-cdb2-5fc9-83e0-82f1953090a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401188} {:answer/id #uuid "b598f6a3-a6bb-5202-8ed8-ac9c42eb7279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401189} {:answer/id #uuid "bca486aa-25ef-5c7c-9609-2ed1a4c5553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401190} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3464e3-f53d-53d8-877c-0141084f3eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401191} {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192} :answer-field/id #uuid "753118cd-0a71-5bb3-aaee-283497ceb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "1d0addec-158f-59f3-a8b0-a1fbeca5ea06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49fc877d-2b64-58a0-a27d-95f261c0a7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276131} {:answer/id #uuid "4db3508a-8d7f-5449-9c40-7069ccf6fd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276162} {:answer/id #uuid "855bca3a-ff78-5604-b854-6c63d00bbe2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276671} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7df95b-b32d-5ca1-b866-576a47074953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeaac23-e098-5ed2-b6ba-88b5f1f7e94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5c8f10-5fa6-51ed-b472-61ec07b1ffb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb96d7dc-7f59-56fa-90d1-c2a71b2fab70" :question/math-academy-id "q-160460" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "85b72007-63eb-5d72-bd12-04d202f52103" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4969b933-9252-5d6c-99a3-2d0800a47ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276127} {:answer/id #uuid "770127d5-5c20-5966-b228-70cc5f941617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276551} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e8d31-b0f2-5bc7-b250-aae11f693050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cce8a1-986b-5e0d-9d7a-5c50318cedd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277250} {:answer/id #uuid "d9290ebb-9e9d-5d8a-8ec2-a3ee34d8fd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111} :answer-field/id #uuid "94528d39-40c7-5d22-bbd3-10b2d8db0c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1077730f-94dd-5875-b9c2-642bfe60bb08" :question/math-academy-id "q-160582" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c3612bc-50a7-5cec-b6e2-2f8735720eb8"}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407320}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433508} :activity/id #uuid "9d89a91b-0f23-5034-8485-cf2fdb766717" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9ce46f-74e5-51fc-85f1-be6a4dc35d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433498} {:answer/id #uuid "144c14ba-18af-5f87-a114-1f0e70bc5c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(-1,-2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433499} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1b9805-9295-5f1f-93ad-1b87d622dbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433500} {:answer/id #uuid "2794a3de-45e3-5d1c-aaa5-e06f453a2bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433501} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eec4e3-df6c-5bff-a85a-74dee074332e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433502} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f46da7-c0a4-56d1-b636-f9591428aeed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ffd9297-ce86-5e32-becf-cbd84b607c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-160476" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=(u-1)^{3}-(v-2)^{3}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u-1)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u-1)^{2} & -3(v-2)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}\\cdot 1-(-3(v-2)^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3(u-1)^{2}+3(v-2)^{2}=0$ defines the point $(1,2)$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "2829a17c-8a17-5054-a69a-9c845f86a147"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "552040e6-e34a-5420-b715-153752988504" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272de190-0e96-5d51-95d4-ff366ae72e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348785} {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} {:answer/id #uuid "93ea44ae-a889-5898-8990-fdfc9599ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349770} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdca57-4e7f-505d-ba98-23b6018b411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350265} {:answer/id #uuid "f22a24d4-0975-5c9c-9994-b66abef9c47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6831b19c-e658-54e3-b4bb-f0560cf60cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a48caee-df37-515d-8b93-7198deb33b28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d467be4-da9c-5949-964e-fc1ece22124b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u+v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402618} {:answer/id #uuid "99d8765a-e6b9-5b6f-b95c-7ee236d3b208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u-v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402619} {:answer/id #uuid "a23b6f7f-749d-5d1a-b619-0c32cdbf023d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} {:answer/id #uuid "128c4bf5-7f24-5412-a170-6a74863be1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402621} {:answer/id #uuid "72a4bc7a-67e1-58b0-9c23-038668c40386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05d1ca1-306f-5d0c-970a-e108a73f246e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48feab89-768b-5b8e-8c0a-02f86fdff070" :question/math-academy-id "q-38245" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -8uv & =0 \\\\ uv & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $uv=0$ defines the pair of intersecting lines $u=0$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane. Notice that these lines correspond to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "df267d9e-6bbe-5490-b116-b3284eaeaf33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd60f7-0060-5908-af95-4c108c3b1718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404165} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5de86-0281-5ef0-9bac-c48c24b2a6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404167} {:answer/id #uuid "90292b65-61da-58e0-bd75-eda1b58d756e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404168} {:answer/id #uuid "22f6e316-1aa7-5c12-abf8-9b28b8e93f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87074f0-9d22-553d-826e-78e5774760d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59e9bc67-c967-5c58-a234-d26582651fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81279" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "e590a33c-a8f9-5732-980b-cf8843af4f45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433507}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433497}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486480} :activity/id #uuid "9906dd6e-f368-5644-ad97-3ccd3454dbd9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f3745d-f09c-5360-a931-21a667dd1d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465453} {:answer/id #uuid "21ed980e-4000-5687-a17b-d84e6f1e4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8bbd34-591c-5d0f-b655-42fc58838887" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835fd9d2-ac5f-5fc7-a341-f53a230e3899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} {:answer/id #uuid "1205f76c-8469-5ede-a981-4f167eb37530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92c5843-42f4-52a3-a501-b15764eb3a62" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36cd71c2-430d-5fd7-a358-b102308c8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} {:answer/id #uuid "71481a29-9e86-5894-95fe-7fecf6d21f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98b628c-ca32-5dd8-8891-b556d9bd4149" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4eb620b0-5fb5-5c19-a1ea-9934c615ac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4b56f991-f12d-5895-8d7a-a6f922386e67"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ef2d77-bba0-583d-ab24-6864e8cb1120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321553} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6dc061-def5-52ec-97ef-1ebc1a91b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321608} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dcda6c-6b89-5eeb-91a4-9597bb3375ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d659d-8fcd-5f7f-88f9-41d43214f667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322957} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d56d-f9b3-57f8-98d4-395a8bb6a219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322563} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae91dc2-4344-5852-b87e-1866ee0bd08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ba03a11-dea1-56d5-9626-63bbe9ed1384" :question/math-academy-id "q-38079" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-2v\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}={u}^{2}$ defines a pair of intersecting lines $v=u$ and $v=-u$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "4da312bf-59b4-5062-8ec0-dbcdcb029a0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121cd8c0-d560-5b26-a5e1-a9a682d56bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348603} {:answer/id #uuid "704f578b-3d6d-5087-b641-678331beb665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349482} {:answer/id #uuid "765f759f-cb7f-500e-a2e6-effae823f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533} {:answer/id #uuid "a195f1c2-37e2-5dd7-8c22-1fa039d5302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} {:answer/id #uuid "b0121869-6909-521a-9b62-122d348331d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbe94b8-88e8-57a5-bae1-d604a1f7732b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7079635f-c699-5683-9c56-da86db0d6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-81319" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f3a2d34-0995-5368-905a-f5f4b05fda88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81738919-7aa7-51e9-a640-81f3dcaec4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383524} {:answer/id #uuid "46ebbeb2-cd8e-59fa-897a-755f09186557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383525} {:answer/id #uuid "21112086-2ed7-5be4-ac2f-eff59a4b2ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383526} {:answer/id #uuid "78535bbb-7c2d-52da-8e93-a0da8a8b205c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383527} {:answer/id #uuid "960bc4eb-19d3-58a0-8107-c7c207282b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383524} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f817ac-86b2-5ffa-80ca-0153f1c9510b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f0fb416-ba54-5520-a3cd-23b891dc09c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-160477" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "85fa7b96-b748-5bcb-8112-da4536f9e0d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9a8a63-07dd-5f16-a2f8-3b7c74281739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485910} {:answer/id #uuid "6b09dc02-1831-530e-86c4-190d1b8a2dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485947} {:answer/id #uuid "7766cddb-20aa-5b50-8013-76522352d29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485971} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5b8a16-21fd-5169-addf-da191e48ae8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486022} {:answer/id #uuid "9673978f-ee00-5dbf-beeb-1fda93353e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0df0bc-bf6e-51c0-bdb6-19b9ad7ef142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5787fa90-c666-5c53-9045-790fb21cdab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160577" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ 3y & 3x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 3x-(-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}})\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =\\frac{9x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8799c879-5fd1-5dbd-8c44-d6fe6d0c6a5b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486477}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485758}]}
